{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router now supports configurable recovery latency modes - AWS","context":"AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router has introduced configurable latency settings, allowing users to control the internal connection latency between inputs and outputs. Previously, the latency inside the router was set automatically and couldn't be adjusted. The router now offers two latency configuration options on router outputs: balanced mode and low-latency mode. Balanced mode maintains the existing behavior for general use cases, while low-latency mode optimizes the internal connection recovery time for latency-sensitive workflows. The latency setting can be configured and viewed using the MediaConnect API, AWS Management Console, or AWS CLI. This feature is available in all regions where MediaConnect Router is deployed. A new CloudWatch metric, RouteFabricRecoveryLatency, provides visibility into the configured recovery latency for each route","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/mediaconnect-router-latency-modes/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"troubleshooting-application-failures","skill_description":"Troubleshoots failing applications by discovering and analyzing CloudWatch log groups to identify error patterns, root causes, and actionable solutions. Use when an application is experiencing failures and log-based diagnosis is needed.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, lambda, api-gateway, ecs\nTasks: troubleshoot, debug, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: monitoring\n*","skill_name":"troubleshooting-application-failures"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS docs on MediaConnect CloudWatch Logs support for flows"}
